  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining Product Types**\n\nI\'m now structuring the application, creating modular components. The first step involves defining the `Product` type within `src/types.ts`. This includes fields like `id`, `name`, `category`, `quantity`, `unit`, `expiryDate`, `barcode`, `location`, `notes`, and timestamps for `createdAt` and `updatedAt`. I\'m also planning to define the `Category` type.\n\n\n**Expanding Data Structures**\n\nI\'ve been working on expanding the data structures. I\'ve defined `ShoppingItem`, `User`, `HouseholdMember`, and `Notification` types. The `ShoppingItem` includes fields for added status and the reason for the item\'s inclusion. The `User` type encompasses preferences like language and units. I am also working on initial data within `src/data/initialData.ts`, including products with realistic dates and categories.\n\n\n**Developing Data Features**\n\nI\'m now building out more data features. I\'m adding a product barcode database dictionary with lookup support, and a fallback to the OpenFoodFacts API. Additionally, I\'m integrating preset household members (including statuses) and shopping list items. I\'m focusing on bilingual i18n support, and planning for a pantry inventory report using `jspdf`.\n\n\n**Designing User Interface**\n\nI\'m now focusing on user interface components. I\'ve been designing the `TopAppBar` with the brand logo, title, notification indicators, a share button, and a sync indicator. I\'m also working on the `BottomNavBar` with tabs for the dashboard, shopping list, and profile. The dashboard will have a search bar and horizontal tab filters to organize products. I\'ve designed a bento grid display and a quick action bar for common tasks.\n\n\n**Implementing Scanner Components**\n\nI\'m now implementing the barcode scanner, starting with `BarcodeScannerModal.tsx`. It will use a real-time camera stream, viewfinder, flashlight toggle, and a \"Simulate Scan\" button. I\'m also adding a manual code input option and immediate feedback. The modal will autofill a product form on successful scan.\n\n\\n\\n\n\n**Constructing Product Forms**\n\nNext, I\'m constructing `ProductFormModal.tsx`. It will have \"Add\" and \"Edit\" modes, with fields for name, category, quantity, unit, location, expiry date, and barcode (with a scanner launcher). I\'ll include save and delete buttons, alongside success toasts and animations. Finally, I\'m working on `ShoppingListView.tsx`, and planning to include a header describing suggestions based on inventory and expiry dates, with a filter.\n\n\n**Designing Shopping List**\n\nI\'m now designing the shopping list user interface. I\'m planning shopping item cards with status indicators and a toggle button. I\'ll include a quick entry box for adding products and a floating action button for list generation and export. I\'ll also add a success modal. I\'m also planning the profile view. This will include an avatar, editable name, email, and \"data backed up\" indicator. I\'m designing Bento navigation cards for account settings, shared household management, and app preferences like dark mode, language, and units. I will also be adding a logout button, and planning the preferences modal and view. This will include a language switcher, appearance switch, units of measure, and a save button.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 6
      nanos: 301138251
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
