    thinking: "**Analyzing Server Setup**\n\nOkay, I\'m deep diving into the integration of Express with the Gemini API, focusing on setup according to framework instructions. I\'m prioritizing the `server.ts` file, especially the build and dev scripts. I\'m trying to align configurations with the framework\'s specifics. I\'m focusing on the structural requirements.\n\n\n**Reviewing Configuration Details**\n\nI\'ve just moved on to examining the `package.json` scripts, specifically the \"dev\", \"build\", and \"start\" commands, and how they relate to the `server.ts` file. I\'m taking a close look at the build process using Vite and esbuild, especially the bundling configuration. I\'m also reviewing the initialization of the Gemini API within the `server.ts` file and its configuration. I\'ve noted the specified model is `gemini-3.7-flash`, suitable for text and structured JSON tasks.\n\n\n**Developing AI Insight Features**\n\nI\'m now fully immersed in developing the \"An\303\241lisis IA\" feature. I\'m focusing on the `AIInsightsView.tsx` component and how it will integrate with the backend via the `POST /api/ai/analyze-trends` endpoint.  I\'m also determining how to structure the AI analysis results in a user-friendly way. My current focus is on creating the appropriate structured JSON to handle product recommendations and insights, considering both high-velocity items and potential waste.\n\n\n**Defining Insight Structure**\n\nI am currently working on defining the structure of the AI-driven insights, particularly for recommendations. I\'m focusing on three key areas. Firstly, I\'m thinking about high-velocity items: identifying items that are frequently consumed and providing proactive replenishment suggestions, including a one-click \"Add to Shopping List\" button. Secondly, I need to consider items at risk of waste, offering clear explanations and recommended actions like reducing purchase size or consuming immediately. Finally, I\'ll incorporate analyses of shopping habits and purchase patterns, including comparisons of categories bought versus wasted, pantry efficiency scores.\n\n\n"
