    thinking: "**Investigating Server Location**\n\nI\'m now looking for the backend by checking for the file `server.ts`. It seems that file isn\'t located in the root directory. I then looked at the `package.json` scripts, and found the dev script looks like this: `\"dev\": \"vite --port=3000 --...\"`.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Backend Framework**\n\nNow I have a better understanding of the backend. The `package.json` scripts are helpful. The \"dev\" script uses `tsx server.ts`, suggesting `tsx` is the runtime for `server.ts`. This file handles `/api/*` endpoints and uses Vite middleware in development. Then, the build script uses `esbuild` for bundling the server. Further investigation shows `express` is used for the backend framework. I have confirmed from dependencies that `tsx`, `esbuild`, and `express` are installed. I\'m now sketching out the `/api/ai/analyze-trends` endpoint for the AI analysis. It will take `products`, `shoppingItems`, `history`, and `preferences` for input.\n\n\n**Defining AI Output Schema**\n\nI\'m now detailing the AI output schema. I\'ll utilize `gemini-3.7-flash` via `ai.models.generateContent`, leveraging `process.env.GEMINI_API_KEY`. The output will be structured JSON using a defined schema and `application/json` mime-type. It covers summary, efficiency score with a grade, items needing refilling (`topBoughtVelocity`), waste risk items, weekly buying forecasts, actionable tips, and quick rescue recipes.\n\n\n**Formulating Error Handling**\n\nI\'m now focusing on error handling, creating fallbacks if the API key is missing. This involves client-safe heuristics and user-friendly error messages. The logic is designed to gracefully degrade without exposing sensitive information. I\'m also adding a new `/api/ai/ask-advisor` endpoint to allow users to ask custom questions, complete with a conversational history context.\n\n**Designing Front-End Components**\n\nI\'m starting on the UI elements. I\'m building an `AIInsightsView.tsx` with a sustainability score dial and cards for high-consumption items. Each card will show product velocity, consumption speed badges, and quick add buttons for shopping.\n\n\n**Detailing UI Action Cards**\n\nI\'m now designing the user interface\'s interaction cards, focusing on direct actions and clear warnings. The new design includes adding items directly to the shopping list with recommended quantities, which triggers a toast notification. I am also working on sections highlighting overbuying risks or near-expiration products. I\'ve included red/orange warning indicators and direct actions such as \"View rescue recipes\" or \"Mark to consume today.\" Lastly, I have sections dedicated to weekly purchase forecasts and zero-waste rescue recipes. I\'m excited by this progress.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
